  "headlines": {
    "governedTokenSavingReplicated": "Governed used 39% fewer tokens than raw, in both pairs: Haiku 14.1M to 8.5M, Gemini 75.0M to 46.0M.",
    "capabilityEqualizer": "Haiku with the governed substrate matched the structural outcome Gemini achieved on raw files, at a fraction of the tokens. Gemini alone maintained the raw site; Haiku alone did not.",
    "exposure": "Time-under-damage (sum of broken references across all 30 snapshots): Haiku raw 724, Haiku governed 4, Gemini raw 69, Gemini governed 4.",
    "predictionScorecard": {
      "brokenReferences": "held \u2014 governed near zero throughout in both pairs; raw model-dependent (Haiku peaked 44, Gemini 6)",
      "styleForks": "reversed for one model \u2014 Haiku governed produced 28 (design system lacked a needed component); Gemini governed produced 4",
      "tokens": "held, exactly, twice \u2014 39% saving in both pairs"
    }
  },
  "caveats": [
    "n = 1 per cell (governed Haiku has an independent reproduction); directional, no significance claimed.",
    "RIFT gained capabilities during the study (write-time link validation, folders, moves) because the benchmark exposed the gaps; pin history records every change.",
    "The governed arm has no page-retirement capability; its remaining structure failure and orphaned pages are substrate refusals, tagged separately in the data.",
    "Cross-model token totals are not comparable (different tokenizers and turn economies); the 39% figure is within-model.",
    "Orphan metrics are reported two ways: no-inbound-content-link (author view) and unreachable-including-navigation (reader view)."
  ]
